generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}


datasource db {
  provider = "mysql"
}


model Users {
  id        Int        @id @default(autoincrement())
  email     String     @unique
  password  String
  firstName String
  lastName  String
  createdAt  DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt  DateTime   @updatedAt
  phone     String     @unique
  role Role
  tasksId Int
  tasks Tasks[] @relation(fields: [tasksId],references: [id])
}


model Projects {
  id          Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  name        String   @unique
  description String
  createdAt    DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt    DateTime @updatedAt
  timeline    DateTime
  status      Status
  tasks       Tasks[]
  createdBy   Int
  updatedBy   Int
}


model Tasks {
  id          Int          @id @default(autoincrement())
  name        String       @unique
  description String
  time        DateTime     @default(now())
  createdAt   DateTime     @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime     @updatedAt
  dueDate     DateTime
  priority    TaskPriority @default(NORMAL)
  projectId   Int
  assignBy   Int
  assignTo   Int
  status      TaskStatus   @default(PENDING)
  project     Projects     @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])


  @@index([projectId], map: "Tasks_projectId_fkey")
}



enum TaskStatus {
 PENDING
 COMPLETED
 FAILED
 INPROGRESS
 CANCELLED
}



enum TaskPriority {
 LOW
 NORMAL
 HIGH
}
enum Role{
  ADMIN
  SUPERADMIN
  USER
}
enum Status {
 ACTIVE
 COMPLETED
 PENDING
}





     what are the flawwsss and drawbacks of this schema and missing things

Yes — there are several important issues in this schema. The biggest problem is relationships: some of your foreign keys are pointing in the wrong direction, and some relations are not actually defined.

For your Mini ClickUp / Task Management SaaS, I would fix the data model before running migrations.

1. Biggest flaw: Users.tasksId is wrong

You currently have:

tasksId Int
tasks Tasks[] @relation(fields: [tasksId], references: [id])

This is structurally incorrect.

You are saying:

One User has a tasksId, but also has many Tasks.

A task should instead contain assignTo, because the task is assigned to a user.

You already have:

assignTo Int
assignBy Int

So the relationship should be:

User 1 ──────────── * Tasks
                    |
                 assignTo

Meaning:

Tasks {
  assignTo Int
  assignee Users @relation(...)
}

There should not be tasksId in Users.

2. assignTo has no relation

You have:

assignTo Int
assignBy Int

but Prisma doesn't know what those IDs refer to.

You need relationships such as:

assignee Users @relation("AssignedTasks", fields: [assignTo], references: [id])
assigner Users @relation("CreatedTasks", fields: [assignBy], references: [id])

And then in Users:

assignedTasks Tasks[] @relation("AssignedTasks")
createdTasks Tasks[] @relation("CreatedTasks")

This is especially important because both assignTo and assignBy point to the same Users table. Named relations are therefore necessary.

3. Projects.createdBy and updatedBy are just integers

You currently have:

createdBy Int
updatedBy Int

But there is no relation to Users.

You want:

User
 ├── createdProjects
 └── updatedProjects

So:

createdByUser Users @relation("CreatedProjects", fields: [createdBy], references: [id])
updatedByUser Users @relation("UpdatedProjects", fields: [updatedBy], references: [id])

And in Users:

createdProjects Projects[] @relation("CreatedProjects")
updatedProjects Projects[] @relation("UpdatedProjects")
4. You're missing ProjectsList / user's projects

You previously wanted users to have a project list.

Right now there is no relationship between:

Users ↔ Projects

except indirectly through createdBy.

But in a ClickUp-like system, multiple users should be able to work on a project:

Project
 ├── User A
 ├── User B
 └── User C

That's a many-to-many relationship.

I recommend a join table:

model ProjectMembers {
  projectId Int
  userId    Int

  project Projects @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])
  user    Users    @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id])

  @@id([projectId, userId])
}

Then:

Users
  ↓
ProjectMembers
  ↓
Projects

This is better than putting something like:

projectsId Int[]

in Users, especially with MySQL/Prisma relational modeling.

5. timeline DateTime is probably insufficient

You have:

timeline DateTime

What does timeline mean?

If a project has:

Start: August 1
End: August 30

one DateTime cannot represent that.

Better:

startDate DateTime
endDate   DateTime

Similarly, tasks already have:

dueDate DateTime

You might eventually want:

startDate DateTime?
dueDate   DateTime?

because a task doesn't necessarily need to have a start date when it's created.

6. Tasks.name @unique is probably a mistake

You have:

name String @unique

That means you cannot have:

Project A
  └── Login

Project B
  └── Login

because "Login" can only exist once in the entire database.

That's usually not what you want.

Remove:

@unique

from task names.

Same question applies to:

Projects.name @unique

Global project-name uniqueness may or may not be desirable.

For a SaaS application, I would generally allow different users/organizations to have projects with the same name.

7. phone @unique may cause problems

You have:

phone String @unique

That's okay if every user must have a unique phone number.

But if phone is optional, it should be:

phone String? @unique

Otherwise every user must provide a phone number.

8. status defaults are missing

You have:

role Role

and:

status Status

These require a value every time a User/Project is created.

You probably want:

role Role @default(USER)

For projects:

status Status @default(PENDING)

For example:

role Role @default(USER)

This also makes your signup API much simpler.

9. Your Project status and Task status are okay, but could be improved

You have:

enum Status {
 ACTIVE
 COMPLETED
 PENDING
}

and:

enum TaskStatus {
 PENDING
 COMPLETED
 FAILED
 INPROGRESS
 CANCELLED
}

This works, but I'd consider whether Project needs FAILED, ARCHIVED, etc.

For example:

enum ProjectStatus {
  ACTIVE
  COMPLETED
  ARCHIVED
  CANCELLED
}

And:

enum TaskStatus {
  TODO
  INPROGRESS
  COMPLETED
  CANCELLED
}

PENDING and TODO can mean almost the same thing, so choose one consistently.

10. You need indexes on foreign keys

You have:

projectId Int

and correctly have:

@@index([projectId])

But you should also index:

assignTo
assignBy
createdBy
updatedBy

For example:

@@index([projectId])
@@index([assignTo])
@@index([assignBy])

This becomes important when your database grows.

11. Missing organization/workspace concept

This is a major architectural consideration for a SaaS.

Your current structure is:

Users
Projects
Tasks

But if this is actually going to behave like ClickUp, you will eventually want:

Organization / Workspace
        |
        ├── Users
        ├── Projects
        └── Tasks

For example:

Acme Company
 ├── Sakshi
 ├── Rahul
 └── Priya

 Projects
 ├── Website
 ├── Mobile App
 └── Marketing

Without a workspace/organization, it becomes harder to support multiple companies using your SaaS.

For your training Mini ClickUp, you can skip this initially. For a production SaaS, I'd add it.

12. Missing soft delete

Consider:

deletedAt DateTime?

for Users, Projects, and possibly Tasks.

Instead of permanently deleting:

DELETE user

you can do:

deletedAt = current time

This is useful for recovery and auditability.

Not mandatory for your MVP.

13. Missing task ordering

For a ClickUp-style task system, eventually you'll want users to control ordering:

Task A
Task B
Task C

You could add:

position Int @default(0)

Then tasks can be reordered without relying on createdAt.

14. Missing comments

If you want this to become a real task-management system, you'll probably eventually need:

Task
 ├── Comments
 │    ├── User A: "Please fix this"
 │    └── User B: "Done"

Something like:

model TaskComment {
  id        Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  content   String
  taskId    Int
  userId    Int
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  task Tasks @relation(fields: [taskId], references: [id])
  user Users @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id])

  @@index([taskId])
  @@index([userId])
}

Not necessary for your current MVP, but very useful later.

15. Missing task attachments/files

Eventually:

Task
 ├── description
 ├── comments
 ├── attachments
 └── assignee

You could later have:

TaskAttachment

with:

id
taskId
fileName
fileUrl
uploadedBy
createdAt

Don't add it unless you actually need it.

16. Missing audit/history

You currently have:

createdBy
updatedBy

That's good, but it only tells you who last changed something.

It doesn't tell you:

August 10:
Sakshi assigned task to Rahul

August 11:
Rahul changed status to INPROGRESS

August 12:
Priya changed priority to HIGH

For a proper SaaS, eventually you might want:

ActivityLog

But again, this can wait.

17. Password field is okay, but remember it must contain hashes

You have:

password String

That's normal.

But your application should never store plain-text passwords.

It should store something like:

$2b$10$...

using bcrypt/argon2.

The schema itself doesn't need a special Prisma type for this.

18. Naming could be more consistent

You currently have:

Users
Projects
Tasks

and singular relation names.

Prisma commonly works nicely with:

model User
model Project
model Task

Then:

users
projects
tasks

However, your current plural model names are not technically wrong.

This is mostly a style/readability issue.

